{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 12 'pending-link failure policy|pending run|pending link|bullet 4|lease' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\"","aggregated_output":"877-A completion is the step-6 result or a refusal, tagged with its link id:\n878-\n879-```clojure\n880-{:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :status :ok :manifest m :derivation d\n881- :image {...} :obligations [...]}\n882-{:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :status :refused :reason :absent :address a}\n883-```\n884-\n885-A link with no completion yet is `:pending`; the caller steps again\n886-when it chooses. Retry cadence, deadlines, and permanent-absence policy\n887-are the composition's, as D6 states: streams report, they do not decide.\n888-`fetch` takes no deadline. A composition that wants one expresses it\n889:with `dao.lease` (`docs/design/dao.lease.md`): its drive holds a lease\n890-over the link and renews it from its own control flow whenever the link\n891-makes progress, and a judge in the same composition, reading a tick\n892:stream it wires, lapses the lease on `:silence` and calls `abandon`,\n893-which completes the link `:lost`. Time reaches this as data, so the\n894-linker stays clock-free.\n895-\n896-`abandon` retires a link's bookkeeping and completes it `:lost` with the\n897-given reason, so a caller that gives up still receives exactly one\n898-completion and never reuses the id.\n899-\n900-### 6.4 `fetch` is host policy over an explicit runtime\n901-\n902-The B6 signature changes in its first argument:\n903-\n904-```clojure\n--\n2302-  with its own `:yin.module/op`, which a later design may take up.\n2303-- **Runtime capability confinement of host modules.** Section 8.3 names\n2304-  the host module as the trusted composition boundary and the capability\n2305-  discipline of `docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md` as the only run-time\n2306-  enforcement. Wiring tokens into effect handlers is engine and\n2307-  composition work outside this document.\n2308-- **SCC identity.** B7 hashes a mutually recursive component as one unit\n2309-  with canonical member ordering. Whether that unit is a manifest of\n2310-  manifests or a single image per format is undecided; section 7.4\n2311-  fixes only the response shape and the cycle refusal.\n2312-- **Failure policy.** Retry, deadline, and permanent absence for a\n2313-  `:pending` link are the composition's (D6). `fetch` takes no\n2314:  deadline; `dao.lease` is the mechanism a composition uses for one\n2315:  (section 6.3). M5 shipped a clock-free pending run with `(abandon)`\n2316-  and no deadline; a configurable `:link-policy` (`:manual` by default, a\n2317:  function for embedders, `:lease` reserved for a `dao.lease` deadline)\n2318-  is designed in `yin.repl.link-policy.md`. The content driver's timing\n2319-  options (`dao.jing.content.driver`, JVM host policy) govern content\n2320-  transport attempts, not the life of a require.\n2321-- **Contract-pinned AST and semantic identities.** Strict decoupling of\n2322-  storage address from VM identity holds for H and R only (section 3).\n2323-  Pinning `:yin.ast/code` and `:yin.semantic/code` identities to a\n2324-  contract hash, with explicit identity-to-address indexes, is an\n2325-  amendment to UCF section 7.3.2 and `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section\n2326-  4.1. The linker is prepared for it: `:identity-matches-fn` and\n2327-  `:indexes` are the only slots that would change.\n2328-- **A stepped DHT client.** The DHT is served behind the content\n2329-  boundary (section 6.1). A portable, non-blocking DHT client that could\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 8 'pending|lease|deadline|expire|timeout' docs/design/dao.lease.md\"","aggregated_output":"1-# DaoLease\n2-\n3:Status: implemented (`src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc`, `test/dao/lease_test.cljc`,\n4:`test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc`; merged 2026-09-20), derived from and\n5-subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). The three use cases in\n6:[`dao.lease.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.lease.implementation-plan.md) are\n7-sketches, not finished components. This document is the operative contract:\n8-every sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\n9:[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\n10-\n11:A lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\n12-contract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\n13-[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\n14-\n15-## Vocabulary\n16-\n17-Facts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\n18:`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\n19:reader switches on both. A fact carrying neither is not a lease fact and is\n20-ignored; a fact carrying both is defective.\n21-\n22-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n23:| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                            | Also required                                                             |\n24-+==========+=======================+=========+=============================================+===========================================================================+\n25:| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask              |\n26-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n27:| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus                    | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`;         |\n28:|          |                       |         | `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one      | `:dao.lease/max` optional                                                 |\n29-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n30:| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | —                                                                         |\n31-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n32:| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | —                                                                         |\n33-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n34:| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\n35-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n36-\n37-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n38:| Evidence   | `:dao.lease/event`   | Author  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\n39-+============+======================+=========+======================+======================+\n40:| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\n41-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n42:| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\n43-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n44-\n45-**Keys.**\n46-\n47:- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\n48-  every fact about it, never reused within that grantor.\n49:- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\n50-  by the grant or refusal answering it.\n51:- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\n52:- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\n53-  medium's attribution takes.\n54:- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\n55:  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\n56-  tolerance use the same representation; tolerance may be zero. A magnitude\n57-  stays within the bound that keeps every comparison and sum of the\n58-  composition's units exact on every host — the reference bound is 2^52 raw\n59-  and, against a supplied table, `quot 2^52 unit-magnitude`; a magnitude past\n60-  it establishes nothing.\n61:- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\n62-\n63-**Units.** The unit keywords and their ratios are a composition's\n64-interoperability decision, fixed once and shared by its grantor, holders and\n65-judge. Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units, and is strict: an\n66-interval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.\n67-\n68-**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when it omits a key\n69-its status or event requires; carries a status or event outside the tables\n70-above; carries a duration, cap or reading that is not a single-entry map of a\n71:fixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\n72-proposal with both `:accepted` and `:rejected`, or repeats `:accepted`,\n73:`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n74:already observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n75-\n76-## Authority\n77-\n78-- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n79-  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n80-  evidence and counts as such.\n81:- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n82-  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n83-  measured as though it had not arrived.\n84-- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n85-  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n86-- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n87-  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n88-  nothing in this vocabulary.\n89-- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n90-  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n91-- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n92-  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n93:  ends the lease.\n94-\n95-## Prohibitions\n96-\n97:- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n98:  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n99-- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n100-- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n101-- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n102-- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n103-\n104-## Time\n105-\n106:Time reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n107-clock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n108-never decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n109-its readings comparable across it.\n110-\n111:A tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n112-under *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n113-\n114-## The judge\n115-\n116-The judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n117-reclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n118-resource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n119-interpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n120-interpreter performing its own act.\n121-\n122:**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n123-\n124-- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n125-- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n126-  changes;\n127-- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n128-  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n129-- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n130:- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n131-  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n132-\n133:A lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n134-and **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n135-appended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n136-\n137-The ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n138-as a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n139-\n140-### The pass\n141-\n142-A pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n143-in this order:\n144-\n145-1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n146-   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n147-   **now** for the whole pass.\n148:2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n149-   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n150:   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n151:   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n152:   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n153-   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n154-   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n155-   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n156:   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n157:   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n158-   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n159-3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n160-   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n161-   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n162:4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n163:   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n164:   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n165:   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n166:   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n167-   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n168:   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n169-   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n170-   resumed reading.\n171:5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n172:   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n173-   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n174:   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n175:6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n176:   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n177:   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n178-   first clause of step 4.\n179-\n180-The record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n181-fact is never evidence of tenure.**\n182-\n183-A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n184-passes. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\n185-step 4.\n186-\n187-### Judging with incomplete evidence\n188-\n189-Absence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n190:`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\n191:and a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\n192-recovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\n193:`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n194-\n195-### Restart\n196-\n197-A grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n198-resource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n199-The reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n200-\n201-This requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n--\n208-  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n209-  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n210-- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n211-  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n212-  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n213-- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n214-  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n215-  whether or not anything has been heard.\n216:- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n217-  it.\n218-- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n219-  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n220-  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n221-  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n222-\n223-For cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n224-which it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n--\n226-extend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n227-requiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n228-\n229-The bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n230-from the resource.\n231-\n232-## Composition duties\n233-\n234:A composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n235-\n236-- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n237-  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n238-- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n239-- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n240-- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n241-  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n242-  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n--\n245-- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n246-- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n247-  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n248-- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n249-  refused at assembly otherwise.\n250-- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n251-  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n252-  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n253:  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n254-\n255-## Sizing\n256-\n257-Stated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n258-\n259-- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n260-- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n261-  its tick stream and the holder's.\n--\n268-\n269-## Limits\n270-\n271-- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n272-  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n273-  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n274-  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n275-  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n276:- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n277-- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n278-  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n279-  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n280-- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n281-  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n282-\n283-## Carriage\n284-\n285:Proposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n286-payload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n287-is delivery, not a second authoring.\n288-\n289-`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n290-remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n291-ordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n292-`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n293-reads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n294-record, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\n295-reclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n296-\n297-## Out of scope\n298-\n299-- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n300:- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n301-- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n302-  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\n303-  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n304-  pair on a meeting peer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
