{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 5 'link-policy|pending|abandon' docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md | head -130\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 5 'link-policy|pending|abandon' docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md | head -130\"","aggregated_output":"1:# yin.repl link policy: what a pending require does when nobody answers\n2-\n3-Status: design, proposed (2026-09-26). Not implemented. Subordinate to\n4-[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) and to\n5-[`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md); it settles the M5 decision that\n6-`yin.vm.linker.md` section 12 (bullet \"Failure policy\") left open. The\n7-mechanism it points at is [`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\n8-\n9-## 1. The problem\n10-\n11-M5 wires `require` at the prompt to the linker. A require whose content\n12:source does not answer stays `:pending`. `yin.repl` parks it as the\n13:shell's `:pending-run`: the prompt returns, lines typed meanwhile are\n14-retained and replayed once, in order, when the link completes, and\n15:`(abandon)` ends the wait and drops the retained lines. Nothing ends a\n16:pending run by itself.\n17-\n18:That is right for a person at a prompt, who can type `(abandon)`. It is\n19-wrong for a session nobody watches: a require against a source that never\n20:answers sits pending forever, and the lines queued behind it never run.\n21-Retry cadence, deadline and permanent absence are the composition's\n22-(`yin.vm.linker.md` D6 and section 6.3), so the policy belongs to\n23-`yin.repl`, and the owner asked that it be configurable.\n24-\n25-## 2. Rulings that bound the design\n26-\n27-- `fetch` takes no deadline. The linker and the engine stay clock-free.\n28-  Time reaches a policy only as data the composition supplies.\n29-- `require` stays an ordinary function. A deadline is a session setting,\n30-  not an argument of `require` and not a new special form.\n31:- The M5 pending run, `(abandon)`, the retained-line replay and the\n32-  identity carry across rollbacks are unchanged. A policy adds one more\n33:  way to reach the abandon path; it adds no second path.\n34-- Rule R and the sealed private resources stand: a policy is composition\n35-  code, never a program value, and no program can reach its state.\n36-\n37-## 3. The option\n38-\n39:`yin.repl/create-state` takes `:link-policy`. It is a session setting kept\n40-on the shell beside `:link-source`, so `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` rebuild the\n41-session without losing it.\n42-\n43-```text\n44-+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n45-| Value     | Meaning                                                |\n46-+===========+========================================================+\n47:| :manual   | The default. Today's behavior: nothing ends a pending  |\n48:|           | run but `(abandon)`. No existing test changes.         |\n49-+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n50-| a function| An embedder's rule, (fn [view]) returning :keep,       |\n51:|           | :abandon or {:abandon reason}. The REPL owns no clock; |\n52-|           | time reaches the function only through what it closes  |\n53-|           | over.                                                  |\n54-+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n55-| :lease    | Reserved for the dao.lease deadline of section 6. In   |\n56-|           | phase 1 it is refused, \"not implemented yet\".          |\n57-+-----------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n58-```\n59-\n60-Any other value is refused at `create-state` with an `ex-info` naming the\n61-supported values. The option fails closed at assembly, not at the first\n62:pending require.\n63-\n64-### 3.1 The view\n65-\n66-The function receives plain data and nothing else:\n67-\n--\n69-{:links [{:name 'foo :link-id [:t0 3]} ...]\n70- :checks n\n71- :lines-retained m}\n72-```\n73-\n74:`:links` are the requests still pending. `:checks` counts the re-checks of\n75:this pending run that made no progress; a re-check that advanced a serve\n76-cursor, delivered a response or completed an install resets the count.\n77-`:lines-retained` is the number of typed lines waiting behind the run.\n78-The view carries no clock reading, no handle and no secret.\n79-\n80-### 3.2 When the policy is consulted\n81-\n82-- when a require parks, with `:checks` 0;\n83:- after each re-check that leaves the run pending.\n84-\n85:It is never consulted when nothing is pending, and never on a re-check\n86-that completes the run: a completion is not a failure.\n87-\n88-### 3.3 What the answers do\n89-\n90-- `:keep` leaves the run as it is.\n91:- `:abandon`, or `{:abandon reason}`, runs the same abandon path as\n92:  `(abandon)`: installs in flight are refused, every link wait entry is\n93-  retired with the reason raised as the require's error, the shell returns\n94-  to the round the require began in, and the link identity minted by the\n95-  parked evaluation is carried onto the base. The reason defaults to\n96:  `:yin.repl/link-policy`; `(abandon)` keeps `:yin.repl/abandoned`.\n97-- The printed message says that the session policy ended the require, in\n98:  the same shape as the message `(abandon)` prints, so a policy abandon is\n99-  never mistaken for a user's.\n100-- Retained lines are dropped once and reported once, exactly as\n101:  `(abandon)` does. No line is evaluated twice, none is lost, and a late\n102:  response to an abandoned link is skipped and cannot settle a later\n103-  require (the M5 identity-carry guarantees hold unchanged).\n104-\n105-### 3.4 A policy that misbehaves\n106-\n107-A function that throws, or returns a value outside the contract, is\n108-treated as `:keep` for that consult and surfaces as one shell error line.\n109:It is never a silent abandon and never ends the session. A broken policy\n110-must not be able to destroy the work it was meant to protect.\n111-\n112-### 3.5 State summary\n113-\n114:`repl-state`'s `:pending` gains the policy name (`:manual` or `:fn`) and\n115-`:checks`, so a host can display why a run is still waiting.\n116-\n117-## 4. Driving a re-check without a typed line\n118-\n119-Today a re-check happens when the user types a line. An unattended host\n120-cannot make a function policy act if nothing triggers a re-check. The host\n121-drivers (`yin/repl/main.cljc`, `yin/repl/driver.cljc`) already own cadence,\n122-so the design question is only whether they can already ask the shell to\n123:re-check a pending run without an input line.\n124-\n125-- If they can, nothing is added; this section is documentation.\n126-- If they cannot, the shell gains one small public step function that\n127-  performs a re-check and consults the policy, and returns the new state\n128-  and the printed text. It reads no clock and takes no callback; the host\n--\n147-is built only when unattended use makes a data-visible deadline necessary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
